THIRD-PARTY DERIVATIVE WORK - THE EEZO SPRITES

All three start from someone else's pixels and none of them are covered by the
public domain dedication in UNLICENSE.

  assets/bertieprogression/textures/block/eezo_ore.png

is a derivative of Mojang's bedrock.png. The ore has to be mistaken for bedrock
at a glance, and a texture merely built to bedrock's palette and histogram still
reads as deepslate, so this one starts from the real pattern: about ten per cent
of its pixels are moved to other positions in bedrock's own palette, and a few
dark violet specks are added. texture-work/make_eezo.py performs both steps and
reads the source from the client jar.

  assets/bertieprogression/textures/item/raw_eezo.png

follows the silhouette of Mojang's raw_iron.png pixel for pixel, recoloured into
the ore's greys and violets. Minecraft assets remain Mojang's, used here under
their terms for modifications of the game.

  assets/bertieprogression/textures/item/eezo_ingot.png

follows the silhouette and per-pixel value structure of AnvilCraft's
titanium_ingot.png, recoloured the same way. AnvilCraft is by Gugle and XeKr and
is distributed under the LGPL-3.0 (https://www.anvilcraft.dev/), which permits
the derivative provided the licence travels with it and the source is named.

Both item sprites are drawn by texture-work/make_eezo_items.py, which holds the
symbol maps and the recolouring tables.

